  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02FD">Proposition <span data-tag="02FD">5.5.3.11</span>.</a>presentable!undercategoriesundercategory!of a presentable $\infty $-categories Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category, and let $p: K \rightarrow \calC $ be a diagram in $\calC $ indexed by a small simplicial set $K$. If $\calC $ is presentable, then the $\infty $-category $\calC _{p/}$ is also presentable. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It follows from Corollary <a href="/tag/BD5D" data-tag="BD5D">5.4.5.16</a> that $\calC _{p/}$ is accessible. It therefore suffices to prove that every diagram $q: K' \rightarrow \calC _{p/}$ has a colimit in $\calC $. We now observe that $(\calC _{p/})_{q/} \simeq \calC _{q'/}$ where $q': K \star K' \rightarrow \calC $ is the map classified by $q$. Since $\calC $ admits small colimits, $\calC _{q'/}$ has an initial object. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>